# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Localization starts with context-rich keys:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: \{\{ch1.rooftop.lavender_greeting\}\}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1.rooftop.lavender_greeting = Good morning.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}menu.settings.volume_label = Volume{/color}
narrator: Keep translator notes near ambiguous lines and never reuse a key for two different meanings.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: stable key names, complete default locale, translated menus, and a missing-key pass before release.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_textkeys
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Text Keys ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_textkeys
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A text key is a placeholder wrapped in double braces:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: {{ch1.greeting}}{/color}
narrator: At runtime, the engine looks up "ch1.greeting" in the active locale file and substitutes the result.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The locale files map each key to actual text:
narrator: {b}en.locale:{/b} {color=#8888cc}ch1.greeting = Good morning!{/color}
narrator: {b}ja.locale:{/b} {color=#8888cc}ch1.greeting = おはようございます！{/color}
narrator: {b}de.locale:{/b} {color=#8888cc}ch1.greeting = Guten Morgen!{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The VNS script never changes. Only the locale files differ between languages.
Lavender: Text keys also work in menu labels, UI strings, and choice button text — anywhere the player sees words.
> Next: locale files -> section_locale_files
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Locale Files ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_locale_files
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: One file per language, stored in the locale directory:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}locale/en.locale{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}locale/ja.locale{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}locale/de.locale{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Format: simple {b}key = value{/b} pairs, one per line:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}# Chapter 1 — Morning Scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1.morning.greeting = Good morning!{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1.morning.farewell = See you later.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}# Menu{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}menu.new_game = New Game{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}menu.load = Load Game{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}menu.settings = Settings{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The engine loads the file matching the player's language setting. If a key is missing, it falls back to the default locale (usually en).
> Next: key naming -> section_naming
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Key Naming ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_naming
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Key naming pattern: {b}category.context.description{/b}
narrator: {color=#66bb88}Good:{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1.rooftop.lavender_greeting{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch2.library.alex_warning{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}menu.settings.volume_label{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ui.confirm.yes{/color}
narrator: {color=#ff6666}Bad:{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}text1{/color}, {color=#8888cc}line_42{/color}, {color=#8888cc}greeting{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Why it matters: a translator seeing "ch1.rooftop.lavender_greeting" knows who speaks, where, and when — without reading VNS code.
Lavender: Generic names like "text1" give zero context. The translation will suffer.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Mirror your script's file structure in your key hierarchy. ch1.* matches chapter_01/*.vns.
> Next: workflow -> section_workflow
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Translation Workflow ────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_workflow
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: The five-step workflow:
narrator: {b}1.{/b} Write scripts using text keys — no hardcoded player-visible text.
narrator: {b}2.{/b} Fill out {b}en.locale{/b} with all text. This is the source of truth.
narrator: {b}3.{/b} Send en.locale to translators. They only edit locale files — never VNS scripts.
narrator: {b}4.{/b} Translators produce ja.locale, de.locale, etc.
narrator: {b}5.{/b} Test each language in-game: check overflow, missing keys, and tone.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The key insight: {b}scripts define structure, locale files define text{/b}. Translators never need to learn VNS syntax.
Lavender: When you add new dialogue, add the key to {b}all{/b} locale files. Mark untranslated keys with "TODO:" for easy searching.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Even single-language games benefit from text keys. They enforce clean separation of text and logic.
> Next: pitfalls -> section_pitfalls
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Pitfalls ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_pitfalls
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Five problems that catch every localized project:
narrator: {b}1. Text length.{/b} German is often 30% longer than English. Japanese is shorter. Design UI with extra space and test early.
narrator: {b}2. Sentence fragments.{/b} Don't concatenate keys to build sentences — word order changes between languages. Each key should be a complete sentence.
narrator: {b}3. Hardcoded text.{/b} Any string written directly in VNS can't be translated. Always use a key.
narrator: {b}4. Missing keys.{/b} A key in en.locale but not ja.locale shows the raw key name to the player. Automate checking for mismatched key sets.
narrator: {b}5. Untranslatable humor.{/b} Puns and idioms don't work across languages. Give translators context notes: "This is sarcastic" or "Pun on X — substitute local equivalent."
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Localization isn't just translation. It's making the game feel native in every language.
> Practice: solve a problem -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A real localization problem. You be the decision-maker.

Lavender: Your game has this line in en.locale:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch3.joke = I'm reading a book about anti-gravity — it's impossible to put down!{/color}
Lavender: The German translator reports: "This pun doesn't work in German. What should I do?"
> Tell them to translate it literally -> practice_literal
> Let them substitute a German pun with the same tone -> practice_adapt
> Remove the joke entirely -> practice_remove

@label practice_literal
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A literal translation loses the humor entirely. The player reads a sentence that doesn't make sense and wonders if the translation is broken.
Lavender: Literal pun translation almost never works. Better to adapt.
[jump practice_done]

@label practice_adapt
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That's the right call. Give the translator a note: "Pun — lighthearted tone, feel free to substitute a local equivalent."
Lavender: The translator knows German humor better than you do. Trust their creative judgment within the tone you set.
[jump practice_done]

@label practice_remove
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Removing it works, but you lose a character moment. Adaptation preserves both the tone and the beat.
Lavender: Only remove content as a last resort — adapt first.
[jump practice_done]

@label practice_done
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Localization rule of thumb: {b}translate meaning and tone, not words{/b}. The player should feel like the game was written in their language.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_14]
[flag tutorial_done_14]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
